<!-- ========== HISTORY ========== -->
<section class="band reveal">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="feature" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--crimson)">Our story</p>
        <h2 style="color:var(--plum);margin-bottom:.9rem">Sixty years of moving, growing, and staying put</h2>
        <p class="lede">Saint Paul has met in a storefront, a shared sanctuary, a historic chapel and an elementary school before finally building a home on Peace Terrace. The address changed a lot. The mission didn't.</p>
        <p style="color:#4a3a63">Today we are a congregation with a majority-Filipino membership worshiping alongside Caucasian, Asian Indian, Ghanaian and other families — still growing, still short on classroom space, still planning the Williams Ministry Center that will give our preschool and Kingdom Kidz room to expand.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tl">
        <div class="tl-item"><span class="tl-year">1963–1964</span><h4>A new church in Newark</h4><p>The California-Nevada Annual Conference starts a congregation; Saint Paul UMC is chartered in November 1964 with 84 members in a storefront on Newark Blvd.</p></div>
        <div class="tl-item"><span class="tl-year">1973</span><h4>Finding our identity</h4><p>Rev. Maurice Cheek moves the congregation to a historic chapel at St. James Episcopal Church in Fremont.</p></div>
        <div class="tl-item"><span class="tl-year">1984</span><h4>Land at last</h4><p>4.4 acres at Lowry Road and Alvarado Blvd purchased together with the Islamic Society of the East Bay.</p></div>
        <div class="tl-item"><span class="tl-year">1990–1991</span><h4>Tri-City Christian Fellowship joins</h4><p>Ministry to the area's Filipino community becomes part of Saint Paul's life. Worship moves to Cabrillo Elementary.</p></div>
        <div class="tl-item"><span class="tl-year">1994</span><h4>Home on Peace Terrace</h4><p>First worship service in the new sanctuary on Easter Sunday.</p></div>
        <div class="tl-item"><span class="tl-year">2001</span><h4>Precious Time Preschool opens</h4><p>Founded by Deaconess Virginia Maniti Williams, still serving Fremont families today.</p></div>
        <div class="tl-item"><span class="tl-year">2026</span><h4>Still here, still welcoming</h4><p>Worship at 10 a.m., dinner at the shelter on Fridays, and a preschool full of two-year-olds.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

